#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f79756-9008-5db9-a58e-f4dda6d20cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185491} {:answer/id #uuid "87633653-db83-5aff-b7bd-240bf6394e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185492} {:answer/id #uuid "d81e8b14-3433-54d9-8503-3e8daadbce31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185493} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0eea0e-e9ed-598c-a0ce-d2a51d1ad5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185494} {:answer/id #uuid "6f95668f-f5da-5049-abb0-85b1ac006401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d81e8b14-3433-54d9-8503-3e8daadbce31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185493} :answer-field/id #uuid "186c6e8a-c524-5e91-8fb3-a11cea8703df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0111779d-e047-5622-b502-1438439ba7f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-110390" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =6xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}6xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}6x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}-0 \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9efbc4f-37b6-5dfc-a3af-79eea3983163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185647} {:answer/id #uuid "041e0497-134b-5de8-bc9b-1ea1cc88a289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.345" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185648} {:answer/id #uuid "16bccea1-d36b-5c0f-b068-3b702c40c2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185649} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9f9576-00aa-5ad8-b83c-03c6b02f7706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.253" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185650} {:answer/id #uuid "79aa0e52-c2f0-5438-9e88-2676450da90c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.423" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c9f9576-00aa-5ad8-b83c-03c6b02f7706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.253" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185650} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf35cc72-3db3-5a32-afbf-f173e83fb7fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01f303ca-e9ff-5d45-9ae0-7430f5f2b5fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143352" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}y, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2).$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}y \\\\  & =y\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\sqrt{x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & ≈0.253.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b3a3fc-340b-5ed5-8d5e-990986db2466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524444} {:answer/id #uuid "411a9cec-3a8e-59f5-9ad3-e086842f889a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524502} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca1210b-5ee2-5865-924d-eb975f41a198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524559} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6c7f06-285b-5716-8654-ada585b5cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524608} {:answer/id #uuid "e274eb34-e730-5088-9d44-3c0f08b82dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f6c7f06-285b-5716-8654-ada585b5cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524608} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b00f6e3-8cc3-5080-b638-0c0a437c40d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1706fef5-2c32-54fe-bdcf-eb9228266f86" :question/math-academy-id "q-143364" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}y, & \\,3\\le y\\le 5, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 2,3\\le Y\\le 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [3,5].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,3\\le y\\le 5 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2,3\\le Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{16}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{b}\\frac{7}{16}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{7{e}^{-x}}{16}]}_{2}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{7{e}^{-b}}{16}+\\frac{7{e}^{-2}}{16}] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a10c141-494b-5ffe-b789-4d235572d460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524600} {:answer/id #uuid "94c5f700-7024-5ad9-9fb8-06de616c6958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524619} {:answer/id #uuid "a350e58a-9fe9-5a48-a78c-f6ab313c004a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524643} {:answer/id #uuid "f085a1c6-cfcd-5341-bcfa-3848ba092d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524805} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb5fdb8-bd3a-507b-bfd0-0f58c8d15589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c5f700-7024-5ad9-9fb8-06de616c6958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524619} :answer-field/id #uuid "54963578-cc23-5bef-b243-20d224d918a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02ed11c8-435c-5d94-883f-47ba8fa464eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143377" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{2}{3}y$ for $y\\in [1,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}y{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(4{x}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "310a997c-e1ee-509f-b5a5-c83f303016e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524471} {:answer/id #uuid "96855423-2c14-53c8-9841-2610cea6223a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524621} {:answer/id #uuid "afa9fea7-5a60-5e00-be15-b60751c4ec57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524665} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d3546d-16f4-553d-bc58-12b528100c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524779} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b9033f-aefa-5a72-bc8f-61f04664c8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afa9fea7-5a60-5e00-be15-b60751c4ec57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524665} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e41b8cd-f547-56b5-b624-9d4998a39501" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09b415e6-d89a-5c4c-91d7-faeef6a95b62" :question/math-academy-id "q-143387" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})$ for $y\\in [2,3]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+yx]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2+2y-\\frac{1}{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [2,3].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=2}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+\\frac{9}{2}-2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [1,2]$ and $y\\in [2,3],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
